Ask what a structural observation distinguishes and what it leaves ambiguous (@chemistry-structure-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-connectivity-spatial-equivalence-and-structural-evidence.lingua#r_008B1AD546865580AD2F407C80") { r_0092A5746E5886E219BCFB5B3E
A structural claim needs an observation relation, reference conditions and alternatives. Reuse [Use a spectrum as chemical evidence with an observation model](../general-chemistry/measurement/Spectroscopic-evidence-and-calibration.lingua#r_5KF8FB97G6AMW8SNB4Y21E2ZHZ) and the earlier chemistry measurement and inquiry blocks. Elemental composition or a molecular formula can constrain identity while leaving constitutional and spatial alternatives. A drawing is a proposed explanation of observations, not itself an observation of every atom. Agreement with one descriptor means only that the candidate lies in that descriptor's fiber.

For a bounded published comparison, [Organic Chemistry tenth edition by John McMurry](../sources/Organic-Chemistry-tenth-edition-by-John-McMurry.lingua#r_00F02946DB1C7E37631D69129A), section 12.7, Worked Example 12.4, distinguishes two C₃H₆O constitutional alternatives through different functional groups. Acetone supplies a carbonyl assignment; prop-2-en-1-ol supplies hydroxyl and alkene assignments. The source associates its illustrative bands with approximately 1715 cm⁻¹ versus 3500 and 1660 cm⁻¹. This is a reading-based comparison of assigned features, not a downloaded spectrum or a new experiment. A functional-group indication does not uniquely identify every structure carrying that group. Mixtures, phase, environment, resolution and interfering signals must be considered before interpreting an apparent match or absence.

The exact geometric example proves another ambiguity without any instrument assumptions: all pairwise distances between four distinctly labeled tetrahedral sites are unchanged by reflection. Any proposed descriptor depending only on those distances therefore cannot identify their handedness. Adding more exact copies of the same distance data does not cure the missing sensitivity. A measurement or interaction sensitive to handedness is a different kind of information, not merely a more precise value of the old descriptor.

Optical rotation provides one such kind of observation, but zero measured rotation does not prove that every molecule is achiral. Read the definition and normalization in section 5.3 and the racemate distinction in the opening of section 5.8. An equal mixture of opposite enantiomers can have canceling rotation. A meso compound and a racemic mixture are different explanations of zero rotation. Calibration, wavelength, temperature, solvent, concentration and other optically active constituents also affect interpretation; a specific-rotation coefficient must retain its stated conditions and unit convention.

For an original, explicitly assumed dilute linear two-enantiomer model, let c₊ and c₋ be mass concentrations in g/mL, l the path in dm, and a>0 the magnitude of the specific rotation in degree·mL/(dm·g) under fixed conditions. Assume opposite coefficients ±a, additive response, no other active constituents and a corrected zero baseline. Then α=la(c₊−c₋). If total concentration c=c₊+c₋ is independently known and positive, d=α/(la) gives c₊=(c+d)/2 and c₋=(c−d)/2. Nonnegative concentrations require |d|≤c. The signed enantiomeric excess in this equal-molar-mass pair is d/c; it refers here to the signs of rotation, not automatically R and S.

Take hypothetical c=0.10 g/mL,l=2 dm,a=10 degree·mL/(dm·g),α=0.4 degree. Then d=0.02 g/mL, c₊=0.06 and c₋=0.04 g/mL, with signed excess 0.20. These are synthetic model values, not properties of lactic acid or any other named chemical. Without the independent c observation, infinitely many nonnegative pairs have the same difference and rotation. With an unknown baseline or a third active component, the two-equation interpretation is again underdetermined. A value |α|>lac contradicts this model and inputs; it must not be repaired by clipping a negative inferred concentration.

**Work:** reconstruct the source's functional-group comparison and list alternatives a single band cannot exclude. Prove the distance-descriptor reflection ambiguity. Solve the synthetic optical account independently, then exhibit a different total concentration with the same observed rotation. Explain why an achiral material, a racemate and an unresolved weak rotation require different follow-up evidence. No sample preparation, separation or laboratory validation is claimed.

